---
import CaseStudy from "../../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import Brief from "../../../components/case/Brief.astro";
import CaseSection from "../../../components/case/CaseSection.astro";
import Diagram from "../../../components/case/Diagram.astro";
import MeasuresDetail from "../../../components/case/MeasuresDetail.astro";
import MeasuresTable from "../../../components/case/MeasuresTable.astro";
import Limit from "../../../components/case/Limit.astro";
import Figure from "../../../components/Figure.astro";
import { pinned, pull } from "../../../lib/sources.ts";
import { measure } from "../../../lib/measures.ts";

const at = (path: string) => pinned("praxis", path);
// Read from the measure, not typed: the description is the one place this figure is shown without <Figure>.
const ratio = measure("praxis.q12_cost_ratio").display.en;
---

<CaseStudy
  no="03"
  name="Praxis"
  repo="praxis"
  summary="A tool that turns a business question into a briefing written by AI models, whose sources are checked by code and whose drafting is reviewed by a model tasked with finding the flaw."
  description={`Praxis: AI-written analysis briefings. Compared blind, on one question, with a single model call ${ratio} cheaper: the reviewer preferred the single call.`}
>
  <Brief
    slot="brief"
    does="From a business question, it searches the web, checks in code that every fact has an acceptable source, then writes a briefing that itself says what it could not source."
    measured="A blind comparison with a single model call, on one question; what a real briefing costs, and how many briefings pass the source check."
    defect="On the one question compared, the reviewer preferred, blind, a single model call that cost far less: Praxis’s value over a single call is unproven. And no real briefing passes the source check."
    figures={[
      { id: "praxis.q12_cost_ratio", caption: "cheaper: the single model call, preferred to Praxis in a blind review (one question, one reviewer)", defect: true },
      { id: "praxis.cost_per_briefing", caption: "cost of a completed Praxis briefing, from the cheapest to the most expensive" },
      { id: "praxis.insufficiently_sourced", caption: "completed briefings flagged as insufficiently sourced", defect: true },
    ]}
  />

  <CaseSection n={1} id="probleme" title="The problem" lead="In two sentences, why this project exists.">
    <p>
      A briefing written by an AI model looks sourced even when it is not. Praxis writes a briefing in a chosen format (briefing paper, memo, position paper) and tells the reader, in the text
      itself, what rests on a verifiable source and what does not.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={2} id="construit" title="What I built" lead="The stages of a briefing, from the question to the delivered file, and the two possible outcomes.">
    <Diagram
      id="praxis-pipeline"
      title="The stages of a Praxis briefing"
      steps={[
        { title: "Scoping", lines: ["what the question covers, which format"] },
        { title: "Web search", lines: ["facts, actors, risks, options:", "every item carries its source"] },
        { title: "Source check, in code", lines: ["missing source, unreliable site,", "page too old, duplicate"] },
        { title: "Drafting", lines: ["one model call per section"] },
        { title: "Adversarial review", lines: ["a model looks for the flaw; it may", "restart the drafting, once at most"] },
        { title: "Delivered file", lines: ["Markdown, PDF, Word"] },
      ]}
      outcomes={[
        { title: "Sourced", lines: ["delivered;", "every item", "has a source"], tone: "proof" },
        { title: "Flagged", lines: ["delivered;", "a section lists", "every item", "without a source"], tone: "defect" },
      ]}
      desc="Praxis first scopes what the question covers and the expected format. A web search gathers facts, actors, risks and options, each with its source. A check written in code rejects missing sources, unreliable sites, pages too old and duplicates. A model then drafts each section, and another looks for the flaw and may restart the drafting once at most. The file is delivered as Markdown, PDF and Word. It is sourced when every item has a source; otherwise it is flagged: a section lists every item without a source."
      caption="Every stage calls an Anthropic model, except the source check, which is code. On the command line and in the test harness, a briefing that fails this check is delivered with a section that lists what it could not source; the run stops if no fact from the search has a source."
    />
    <p>
      The instructions given to the models are <code>.prompt</code> files read by PromptLang. The section of unsourced items is written by the code from the check’s report: no model can soften it.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    n={3}
    id="mesures"
    title="Results"
    lead="What the real, paid runs give, and what is not measured at all."
  >
    <p>
      Of the real runs, <Figure id="praxis.live_completed" where="prose" /> reached the end; a completed briefing cost <Figure id="praxis.cost_per_briefing" where="prose" />. Every completed briefing is
      flagged as insufficiently sourced: <Figure id="praxis.insufficiently_sourced" where="prose" defect />. The flag is raised as soon as a single item blocks.
    </p>
    <h3>A single reference point</h3>
    <p>
      On one question, Praxis was compared blind with a single model call, given the same format and the same search tool, <Figure id="praxis.q12_cost_ratio" where="prose" defect text="9.8× cheaper" />: the
      reviewer preferred the single call (details in the limits below). The only score published earlier rated simulated briefings, written by hand for the tests: it measured those examples, not a model, and
      it was withdrawn (<a href={at("CHANGELOG.md")}>changelog</a>). Outside this comparison, the quality of the briefings has not been reviewed for any question; only the first-hand sources of another
      question were reviewed by a human (<a href={at("benchmarks/outputs/live/README.md")}>real runs</a>).
    </p>
    <MeasuresDetail>
      <MeasuresTable
        where="live"
        caption="Real, paid runs"
        rows={[
          {
            id: "praxis.live_completed",
            label: "Completed briefings, out of the attempts",
            noInterval: "The repository publishes none: the attempts span four versions of the code, and five left no record.",
            reading:
              "The other attempts failed: source check too strict, text before the structured response, time limit exceeded, truncated response, section refused.",
          },
          {
            id: "praxis.insufficiently_sourced",
            label: "Briefings flagged as insufficiently sourced",
            defect: true,
            noInterval: "The repository publishes no interval for this count.",
            reading: "A defect, beside the blind comparison: every completed briefing is flagged. The flag is raised as soon as a single item blocks.",
          },
          {
            id: "praxis.cost_per_briefing",
            label: "Cost of a completed briefing",
            noInterval: "The range of the completed runs, from the cheapest to the most expensive: not an estimate.",
            reading: "Cost recorded with every completed run.",
          },
        ]}
      />
    </MeasuresDetail>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={4} id="echecs" title="Known limits and next steps" lead="Four limits observed on real runs, each with its scope and its next step.">
    <Limit title="No better than a single call, compared blind">
      <Fragment slot="scope">One real question, a sodium-ion battery plant in Morocco; one briefing per approach; one reviewer, the author of Praxis, not told which briefing came from Praxis. The single call searched the web less, and later: the web may have changed between the two.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="limit">
        A single model call, given the same format and the same search tool, cost <Figure id="praxis.q12_cost_ratio" where="limits" defect text="9.8× less" /> than Praxis. The reviewer preferred it, as clearer and more
        useful for a decision; among the sourced sentences checked, more were supported by their source in the single call. According to the reviewer, the grades were recorded before the key matching briefings to approaches was opened (<a
          href={at("benchmarks/review/q12/grades.md")}>reviewer’s grades</a
        >). Under the rule written before the trial, Praxis’s value over a single call is unproven. The result holds for this question only, and the blinding cannot hide the style of Praxis from its author.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="next">More questions, and removing Praxis’s stages one at a time to measure what each adds, as the <a href={at("README.md#status")}>repository</a> states; the <a href={at("docs/preregistration/single-call-baseline.md")}>rule written before the trial</a> has this result published with the cost of both briefings.</Fragment>
    </Limit>
    <Limit title="Real briefings still under-sourced">
      <Fragment slot="scope">Real runs, on a real model, after a development carried out on hand-written examples.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="limit">
        The first real runs all failed; since an independent audit and fixes made without any paid call, <Figure id="praxis.live_completed" where="limits" /> attempts reached the end, and all <Figure
          id="praxis.insufficiently_sourced"
          where="limits"
          defect
          text="6"
        /> completed briefings are flagged as under-sourced by Praxis’s check.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="next">The last part of the <a href={at("ROADMAP.md")}>fix plan</a>: a sample of real briefings scored by a judge validated against human ratings.</Fragment>
    </Limit>
    <Limit title="The strict check threw all the work away">
      <Fragment slot="scope">The strict mode of the source check.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="limit">
        A single item without a source cancelled every stage already done. No trace of those runs was kept: the cause is read in the instruction and the code, not observed. The search instruction required
        a consultation date that the model cannot know; Praxis now dates its consultations itself, which removes the probable cause without guaranteeing a source for every item, and the briefing is
        delivered with its gaps written into it (<a href={at("docs/adr/0002-degrade-on-insufficient-sourcing.md")}>ADR 0002</a>).
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="next">Observing the cause on real runs that are kept, in the same last part of the fix plan.</Fragment>
    </Limit>
    <Limit title="A section refused for a web address in its text">
      <Fragment slot="scope">The adversarial review of the summary sections.</Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="limit">
        A section was refused, then refused again on the retry, this time for a <code>www.</code> address in the text, which document readers turn into a link. The fix (<a href={pull("praxis", 44)}
          >#44</a
        >) replaces that instruction with numbered references to the sources, checked by the code; no paid retry was run only to verify it.
      </Fragment>
      <Fragment slot="next">A published real run that goes through this section.</Fragment>
    </Limit>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection
    n={5}
    id="decisions"
    title="Three decisions"
    lead="Three architecture choices, each written in a decision record (ADR) with its reasons and consequences: enough to judge the reasoning, not only the result."
  >
    <ol class="decisions">
      <li>
        <h3>Read the structured response in code, repairing nothing</h3>
        <p>
          Anthropic’s interface refuses responses in an imposed format when the web search cites its sources, and refuses to force a tool call on the models used. For the stages with search, the
          code keeps the text after the last tool call and extracts from it the largest complete and readable structured response (a JSON object), repairing nothing; the stages without search impose
          their format. In every case, the code validates each field.
        </p>
        <p><a href={at("docs/adr/0001-getting-json-from-the-model.md")}>ADR 0001</a></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Deliver with a flag rather than give up</h3>
        <p>
          On the command line and in the test harness, a failed source check is recorded, the run continues, the report is flagged and the code adds a section that lists every blocking item. The briefing is
          complete, but says in its own text what it could not source. Used as a library, Praxis still gives up by default: changing the errors a public function can raise would break the code of
          those who call it.
        </p>
        <p><a href={at("docs/adr/0002-degrade-on-insufficient-sourcing.md")}>ADR 0002</a></p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <h3>Which sources to trust</h3>
        <p>
          A company’s website is accepted for that company’s facts only, provided that the mapping between organisations and websites is explicit, reviewed and versioned. After a human review, the rule
          never again accepts a first-hand source for the likelihood or impact of a risk. The second part, the specialist press, is not implemented.
        </p>
        <p><a href={at("docs/adr/0003-source-trust-criteria.md")}>ADR 0003</a></p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={6} id="construction" title="How it is built" lead="For a technical reader: the stack, the models used and the test tools.">
    <p>
      TypeScript on Bun. Anthropic’s Messages API, with its web search tool: <code>claude-opus-5-5</code> for scoping, options and the adversarial review, <code>claude-sonnet-5-5</code> for search, actors, risks and drafting. <code>@promptlang/core</code> from npm, pinned by its hash; pdfkit for the PDF; the Word file is assembled by the project’s own code, without a library. Tests: <code>bun test</code>, fast-check, Stryker. Architecture: <a href={at("docs/architecture.md")}>docs/architecture.md</a>.
    </p>
  </CaseSection>

  <CaseSection n={7} id="suite" title="What comes next" lead="What is missing, according to the project’s roadmap.">
    <ul>
      <li>A real run that completes reliably, with its measurements: cost, volume of text processed, duration, rated sample.</li>
      <li>The quality of real briefings rated by a judge model, validated against human ratings, with its margin of error.</li>
      <li>A source’s age measured from the date of the page itself.</li>
      <li>A web search that filters its results along the way, evaluated against the base tool.</li>
    </ul>
    <p><a href={at("README.md#roadmap")}>Roadmap</a></p>
  </CaseSection>
</CaseStudy>
